---
import AppLayout from '../../layouts/AppLayout.astro';
import Window from '../../components/Window.astro';
import ChevronLeft from '../../components/ChevronLeft.astro';
import MobileTitlebar from '../../components/MobileTitlebar.astro';
import '../../styles/mobile-case-study.css';

const sections = [
  { id: 'problem', label: 'Problem' },
  { id: 'bet', label: 'The Bet' },
  { id: 'assistant', label: 'The assistant' },
  { id: 'control', label: 'Control' },
  { id: 'constraint', label: 'The constraint' },
  { id: 'result', label: 'Result & reflection' },
  { id: 'visuals', label: 'More Visuals' },
];
---

<AppLayout
  title="Photoshop 2030 · Ishan Rajpurohit"
  description="Reimagining Photoshop as a 2030 AI editor you can talk to, an Adobe Inter-IIT Tech Meet case study by Ishan Rajpurohit."
>
  <Window title="Work" height={738} transitionName="active-window">
    <ChevronLeft href="/work" />
    <div class="cs-body">
      {sections.map((s, i) => (
        <input type="radio" name="ph-tab" id={`ph-${s.id}`} class="cs-radio" checked={i === 0} />
      ))}

      <div class="cs-row">
        <nav class="cs-sidebar">
          {sections.map((s) => (
            <label for={`ph-${s.id}`} class="cs-pill">{s.label}</label>
          ))}
        </nav>

        <div class="cs-content">
          <div class="cs-panel panel-problem">
            <div class="overview-header">
              <h1 class="overview-title">Reimagining Photoshop as a 2030 AI editor you can talk to.</h1>
              <div class="meta-grid">
                <div class="meta-col">
                  <p class="meta-label">role</p>
                  <p class="meta-value">Sole Product<br />Designer</p>
                </div>
                <div class="meta-col">
                  <p class="meta-label">context</p>
                  <p class="meta-value">Adobe | Inter-IIT Tech Meet 14.0 | AI editor of 2030</p>
                </div>
                <div class="meta-col">
                  <p class="meta-label">team</p>
                  <p class="meta-value">6: design (me), product (1), dev (2), AI (2)</p>
                </div>
                <div class="meta-col">
                  <p class="meta-label">result</p>
                  <p class="meta-value">3rd highest score in design &amp; UX; and 6th of 23 IITs (overall, across design + ML)</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <p class="overview-desc">Adobe&rsquo;s brief: design a lightweight, mobile-first AI image editor for 2030 that stays fast on low-compute devices and keeps the human in control. I was the sole designer on a six-person team. We placed 6th of 23 IITs.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="problem-row">
              <div class="section-text">
                <div class="section-heading">
                  <h2>Problem</h2>
                  <p class="section-sub">By 2030, editing moves to the phone, and to conversation.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="section-body">
                  <p>Photoshop&rsquo;s users have always relied on desktop-class hardware and dense toolbars.</p>
                  <p>Adobe&rsquo;s brief looked ahead: by 2030, creators will expect mobile-first, AI-assisted editing on limited compute, where you say &ldquo;brighten only the left half of the sky&rdquo; instead of hunting for a tool.</p>
                  <p>The hard part Adobe named explicitly: <span class="inline-emphasis">keep the human in control while using small, efficient models.</span> The user has to see what the AI did and be able to refine it.</p>
                  <p>The obvious response is to bolt AI onto the existing tool (Photoshop), plus a prompt box. I didn&rsquo;t want that.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="phone-shot-empty ph-hero">
                <video src="/images/work/photoshop2030/problem.mp4" poster="/images/work/photoshop2030/problem-poster.jpg" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="auto" />
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="cs-panel panel-bet centered">
            <div class="bet-stack">
              <div class="section-text">
                <div class="section-heading">
                  <h2>The bet</h2>
                  <p class="section-sub">I didn&rsquo;t want Photoshop 2030. I wanted an app that feels two-way.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="section-body">
                  <p>Most AI editors work like a vending machine: type an input, get a result.</p>
                  <p>My stand was that the 2030 editor should feel like a <span class="inline-emphasis">conversation layered on top of fast direct actions</span>: you talk to it when you want guidance, and go direct when you don&rsquo;t.</p>
                  <p>The product should feel alive and context-aware, not like a form you submit.</p>
                  <p>That single decision drove these two things: <span class="inline-emphasis">Orby</span>, an assistant that makes the app a partner rather than a passive tool, and a <span class="inline-emphasis">home experience</span> built to feel like a 2030 AI product, not a phone-sized copy of the desktop app.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="info-grid">
                <div class="info-col">
                  <p class="info-heading">Intent over tools</p>
                  <p class="info-body">You describe what you want; the app figures out which operation that is.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="info-col">
                  <p class="info-heading">Human in the loop</p>
                  <p class="info-body">You always see what changed and can refine it. AI assists, it doesn&rsquo;t decide.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="info-col">
                  <p class="info-heading">Conversation on top of speed</p>
                  <p class="info-body">Talk to it for guidance; bypass it entirely when you already know.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="phone-row">
              <div class="phone-shot-empty ph-tall">
                <video src="/images/work/photoshop2030/bet-left.mp4" poster="/images/work/photoshop2030/bet-left-poster.jpg" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="auto" />
              </div>
              <div class="phone-shot-empty ph-tall">
                <video src="/images/work/photoshop2030/bet-right.mp4" poster="/images/work/photoshop2030/bet-right-poster.jpg" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="auto" />
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="cs-panel panel-assistant">
            <div class="section-text">
              <div class="section-heading">
                <h2>The assistant</h2>
                <p class="section-sub">Orby is a guide you summon, not an assistant that interrupts.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="section-body">
                <p>Orby is what makes the app feel human: an emotive navigator that gives context-aware suggestions (&ldquo;Remove tourists&rdquo;, &ldquo;Fix lighting&rdquo;) instead of leaving you to find the right tool.</p>
                <p>But an always-active AI companion is one wrong move away from Clippy: charming in a demo, exhausting in daily use.</p>
                <p>So Orby earns its place by staying out of the way. It sits <span class="inline-emphasis">minimised at the edge of the screen, blinking,</span> and only comes forward when touched or called.</p>
                <p>And it&rsquo;s never a gate: <span class="inline-emphasis">Prompt is direct-to-result, and Stylize and Auto-Enhance are one tap</span>, so a power user can bypass Orby entirely.</p>
                <p>It&rsquo;s a guide for when you can&rsquo;t express what you want, not a toll you pay on every edit.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="phone-shot-empty ph-tall">
              <video src="/images/work/photoshop2030/assistant.mp4" poster="/images/work/photoshop2030/assistant-poster.jpg" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="auto" />
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="cs-panel panel-control">
            <div class="section-text">
              <div class="section-heading">
                <h2>Control</h2>
                <p class="section-sub">Two ways to remove the birds: quick, or precise.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="section-body">
                <p>&ldquo;Human in the loop&rdquo; only means something if the user can choose how much control they want. Ask to remove the birds from a photo and Orby offers two paths.</p>
                <p><span class="inline-emphasis">The Prompt tab:</span> just type it, done.</p>
                <p><span class="inline-emphasis">The Select tab:</span> mask the birds with stylus or touch and erase or inpaint, for precise results. Same goal, two levels of control, the user&rsquo;s call.</p>
                <p>That logic shapes the whole editor. I reduced the cluttered toolbar to a <span class="inline-emphasis">floating capsule of four decisive options</span>: Select (one linear workflow that absorbs Heal, Patch and Move, where you select and then pick the intent: Erase, Move, or Inpaint), Prompt (conversational generation), Edit (legacy manual tools, tucked away), and Layers (non-destructive control).</p>
                <p>AI is the default. Manual is always one tap away.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="phone-shot-empty ph-control">
              <video src="/images/work/photoshop2030/control.mp4" poster="/images/work/photoshop2030/control-poster.jpg" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="auto" />
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="cs-panel panel-constraint centered">
            <div class="section-text">
              <div class="section-heading">
                <h2>The constraint</h2>
                <p class="section-sub">On-device or cloud: the user&rsquo;s choice, not a hidden default.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="section-body">
                <p>The brief&rsquo;s hard constraint was low compute. Most solutions would hide that decision in the backend. I made it a visible toggle: Atom runs locally, private and fast on limited hardware, while PhotoFlux V30 runs in the cloud for high-fidelity results.</p>
                <p>The constraint becomes a feature: privacy and speed when you want them, fidelity when you need it, and the user always knows which one they&rsquo;re using.</p>
                <p>Two smaller calls round it out. One-tap generative shortcuts (Stylize, Auto-Enhance) sit in the top navigation for instant access.</p>
                <p>And every exported, AI-generated layer carries C2PA content credentials: provenance built in, because a 2030 AI tool should be honest about what it made.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="phone-row">
              <div class="phone-shot"><img src="/images/work/photoshop2030/constraint-screen3.png" alt="Photoshop 2030 on-device vs cloud toggle" /></div>
              <div class="phone-shot"><img src="/images/work/photoshop2030/constraint-screen4.jpg" alt="Photoshop 2030 content credentials" /></div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="cs-panel panel-result centered text-only">
            <div class="section-text">
              <div class="section-heading">
                <h2>Result &amp; reflection</h2>
                <p class="section-sub">3rd highest score bracket in Design &amp; UX. Overall 6th of 23 IITs, and a sharper view of AI-UX.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="section-body">
                <p>We got placed in the 3rd highest score bracket in design &amp; UX. And for the overall, AI + Dev + Design Problem Statement, 6th of 23 IITs.</p>
                <p>It was a team result across three tracks (product design, market research, and building the AI pipelines), and I led the product design. This is a concept built under competition time pressure, not a shipped product.</p>
                <p>What I took from it: the hard part of designing for AI isn&rsquo;t the generation. It&rsquo;s <span class="inline-emphasis">control and trust.</span></p>
                <p>The decisions that mattered weren&rsquo;t the impressive outputs; they were the off-ramps: letting the user bypass Orby, the precise Select path next to the quick Prompt one, the visible model toggle.</p>
                <p>Designing where the human stays in control taught me more than any single AI feature could.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="cs-panel panel-visuals centered">
            <div class="section-heading visuals-heading">
              <h2>More Visuals</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="phone-shot-empty ph-full">
              <video src="/images/work/photoshop2030/more-visuals-1-cover.mp4" poster="/images/work/photoshop2030/more-visuals-1-poster.jpg" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="auto" />
            </div>
            <div class="phone-row">
              <div class="phone-shot-empty ph-tall">
                <video src="/images/work/photoshop2030/visuals-2.mp4" poster="/images/work/photoshop2030/visuals-2-poster.jpg" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="auto" />
              </div>
              <div class="phone-shot-empty ph-tall">
                <video src="/images/work/photoshop2030/visuals-3.mp4" poster="/images/work/photoshop2030/visuals-3-poster.jpg" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="auto" />
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Window>

